## Project Harness

## Development-Harness Boundary

`.harness/` is an outer repository-development control plane for coding-agent work, verification, and fresh-task continuity. It is not application runtime and must not be imported by, deployed with, or used as an orchestration dependency of the product.

Any in-product agent, loop, scheduler, or orchestrator is product-owned runtime. It must keep separate state, configuration, telemetry, and operations and must never depend on, read, or write `.harness/`. The development harness may build and test that product code as ordinary feature-scoped code, but it must not absorb or replace the product runtime's lifecycle.

Before changing files, run `node .harness/run.mjs session` and inspect `.harness/continuity.json`. If it reports `awaiting_resume`, run the exact `node .harness/run.mjs resume <handoff-id>` command only from a genuinely fresh chat or task; otherwise stop and open one. Then read `.harness/config.json`, `.harness/features.json`, `.harness/progress.md`, `.harness/handoff.md`, and the task-relevant routes in `.harness/docs-map.md`. Run `doctor`, `status`, and `next` after any required resume.

Work on one active feature by default. Confirm any repository-defined product, security, rights, provider, or deployment gates before implementation. Use `.harness/run.mjs` for state changes. A feature may become `passing` only through successful `verify` output recorded under `.harness/evidence/`. Use bounded `checkpoint` snapshots during a task. End with a dirty-safe, terminal `handoff --summary ... --next ...`; after `STOP_CURRENT_CHAT`, do not edit, resume the transcript, or use context compaction as a substitute. The runtime parks harness mutations and prints a bootstrap but cannot universally create or terminate platform chats. Codex's project-local `PreCompact` hook is a reviewed/trusted defense in depth, not a replacement for this lifecycle.
